5. ITEM MASTER AND PRODUCTION STAGES

Every item must have:

- Unique item code.
- Description.
- Unit of measurement.
- Active/inactive status.
- Optional barcode or QR code.
- Baseline manufacturing cost before trimming.
- Expected production capacity in pieces per hour.
- Optional workstation-specific production capacity.
- Applicable trimming rate or rates.
- QC requirements.
- Rework rules.
- Production-stage information.

The user has specified separate item codes for untrimmed and trimmed items.

For example:

- MLD-001: Plastic Housing A — Untrimmed.
- TRM-001: Plastic Housing A — Trimmed.

The application must support relationships between the untrimmed item, intermediate items where needed, and the final trimmed item.

Do not assume that all products have the same number of stages.

Some products may require one trimming stage; others may pass through several stages.

Important rate rule

A trimming rate is a financial amount per piece.

Expected production capacity is a production quantity per hour.

These are two separate concepts.

For example:

- Trimming rate: Rs. 2 per piece.
- Expected production: 120 pieces per hour.

Never confuse these values.

Stage versus chargeable operation

Production stages describe the progress of an item toward its final state.

A stage transition must not automatically create a trimming charge.

Only actual, recorded, chargeable work operations should create cost or payment entries according to the configured rules.

Support item-specific routing and operation-level tracking where required.

