11. PRODUCTION PLANNING

The system must support workstation-wise planning for employees and in-house vendors.

Initially support workstations WS-01 through WS-70, with the ability to add, rename, deactivate, and configure additional workstations.

Planning inputs

The planner must see:

- Available untrimmed stock.
- Stock reserved for existing work orders.
- Item.
- Applicable production stage.
- Workstation.
- Assigned person.
- Shift.
- Net shift hours.
- Expected production capacity in pieces per hour.
- Planned quantity.
- Outstanding work.
- Availability and downtime.
- Current QC and rework backlog.

Production target

Calculate the suggested production target using:

Expected pieces per hour × available productive hours.

The user must be able to override the suggested quantity with an appropriate reason.

Stock-shortage behavior

The planner chooses how to handle insufficient stock.

The application must clearly show shortages and must never reserve more physical stock than is available.

Allow a production plan to express demand greater than current availability, but flag the shortage.

Assignment rules

Prefer one item per workstation per shift.

Prevent accidental double assignment of the same physical stock.

Normally permit one person per workstation per shift, but allow authorized exceptions.

Permit the same person to work at different workstations in different non-overlapping shifts.

Track employee and workstation availability.

Shift configuration

Support configurable shifts with:

- Name.
- Start time.
- End time.
- Break duration.
- Net working hours.
- Working days.
- Effective date.

Support shifts that cross midnight.

Outsourced vendor planning

Record the vendor's workforce count and available working hours.

Calculate suggested capacity from configured item/operation capacity and available worker-hours.

Allow multiple open job orders for a vendor.

Show outstanding quantities, available capacity, overdue orders, and historical performance.

